# Env file for the BioCypher KG Console stack. Copy to docker/console.env and set the REQUIRED values.

# ── REQUIRED ──
# Absolute host dir for build output + archives; mounted same-path into every service.
DATA_ROOT=/absolute/path/to/kg-data
# Neo4j password (min 8 chars).
NEO4J_PASSWORD=change_me_min_8_chars

# ── EXTERNAL INSTANCES (direct URL; highest priority) ──
# Set these to point the Console straight at an external Neo4j/MORK. When set, up.sh
# uses them verbatim: no port probing, no bundled service. This is the deploy path
# for connecting to an existing external database.
#NEO4J_URI=bolt://neo4j.example.com:7687
#MORK_URL=http://mork.example.com:8027
# (also set NEO4J_USER / NEO4J_DATABASE below if they differ from the defaults)

# ── ATTACH TO EXISTING INSTANCES (optional; used by up.sh when NEO4J_URI is not set) ──
# Convenience alternative to NEO4J_URI: give just the host and up.sh builds the URL.
# Leave HOST blank for this host; set to a remote machine to attach (up.sh can't create one remotely).
#NEO4J_HOST=                # blank = this host; or e.g. neo4j.internal / 10.0.0.5
#MORK_HOST=                 # blank = this host; or e.g. mork.internal / 10.0.0.6

# ── REACH AN EXTERNALLY-MANAGED DB ON THIS HOST (shared docker network) ──
# All host ports are bound to 127.0.0.1 for security, so on Linux a container CANNOT
# reach a DB via host.docker.internal (traffic arrives on the docker bridge, not
# loopback). To reach a Neo4j/MORK the Console does not manage, share a docker network
# with it and address it by SERVICE NAME instead of a host port:
#   1. Put the external DB on a named network (mark it external with a fixed name in
#      its own compose, or run:  docker run --network labdb --name my-neo4j ... ).
#   2. List that network here so up.sh joins the Console to it (creates it if absent):
#CONSOLE_EXTRA_NETWORKS="labdb"
#   3. Point the URLs at the DB's service/container name and INTERNAL port:
#NEO4J_URI=bolt://my-neo4j:7687
#MORK_URL=http://my-mork:8027
# Keeps every host port loopback-only and works the same on Mac and Linux.
# NOTE: with SPECIES_INSTANCES set, up.sh uses the per-species endpoints and ignores
# NEO4J_URI/MORK_URL — comment SPECIES_INSTANCES out to test a single external pair.

# ── PER-SPECIES INSTANCES (optional) ──
# Neo4j Community can't host a database per species, so each species gets its own
# Neo4j + MORK instance. A build for a species then loads into that species' pair,
# and Explore reads it. Two ways to set this up:
#
# 1) LET up.sh CREATE THEM. List the species and up.sh provisions a Neo4j + MORK
#    each (own host ports + volumes) and wires the Console to them automatically.
#    Host ports = base + index in the list (hsa->7687/7474/8432, dmel->7688/...).
#SPECIES_INSTANCES="hsa dmel"
#NEO4J_BOLT_BASE=7687       # first species' Neo4j bolt host port (then +1 per species)
#NEO4J_HTTP_BASE=7474       # first species' Neo4j browser host port
#MORK_PORT_BASE=8432        # first species' MORK host port
#
# 2) POINT AT EXISTING INSTANCES you run yourself. Set the endpoints per species.
#    There is NO cross-species fallback: a species with no endpoint has no instance,
#    so it can't be loaded and won't appear in Explore (by design, so one species'
#    data is never shown under another). Neo4j creds are shared across instances.
#    If these are containers on this host, also set CONSOLE_EXTRA_NETWORKS (above)
#    and use their service names, e.g. bolt://neo4j-hsa:7687.
#NEO4J_URI_HSA=bolt://localhost:7687
#MORK_URL_HSA=http://localhost:8432
#NEO4J_URI_DMEL=bolt://localhost:7688
#MORK_URL_DMEL=http://localhost:8433

# ── OPTIONAL (defaults shown; uncomment to change) ──
#CONSOLE_PORT=9000          # Console UI + API           → http://localhost:9000/console/
#NEO4J_HTTP_PORT=7474       # Neo4j Browser              → http://localhost:7474
#NEO4J_BOLT_PORT=7687       # Neo4j bolt — probed by up.sh; also the bundled host port
#MORK_HOST_PORT=8432        # MORK port — probed by up.sh; also the bundled host port
#NEO4J_PAGECACHE=1G         # Neo4j memory — raise on hosts with more RAM (e.g. 4G)
#NEO4J_HEAP_MAX=1G
#NEO4J_USER=neo4j
#NEO4J_DATABASE=neo4j
#NEO4J_IMAGE=neo4j:5.21
#MAX_CONCURRENT_BUILDS=1
#MAX_OUTPUT_BUILDS=5        # keep the newest N build output folders per writer+species
#BUILD_MAX_WORKERS=1        # parallel adapter workers per build. 1 = sequential (safe on
                            # low Docker memory). Raise (e.g. 4) only if Docker has plenty
                            # of RAM; too many workers OOM the build (BrokenProcessPool).

# ── Neo4j / APOC flags the stack applies (override only if needed). ──
#NEO4J_APOC_EXPORT_FILE=true
#NEO4J_APOC_IMPORT_FILE=true
#NEO4J_APOC_IMPORT_USE_CONFIG=false
